S7: believe and answer only the local link, and bound the replies

mdns-sd reads every datagram that reaches port 5353 on an enabled
interface and acts on it, whatever its source:

- A query from any address is answered. One whose source port is not
  5353 gets a legacy-unicast reply (RFC 6762 §6.7) sent straight to that
  address and port, which the kernel routes off the link if it has to;
  such replies are exempt from the once-per-second multicast limit, so a
  stream of 50-byte queries with a spoofed source makes the responder an
  unthrottled reflector of 300-byte answers carrying the device name and
  port.
- A response from any address and any source port is believed and cached.
- Deferred PTR responses queue without limit.
- The legacy-unicast test compares the source port with the constant
  5353, not the daemon's own port, so two daemons on another port (tests,
  development) answer each other by unicast.

Now a packet whose source is neither link-local nor on a subnet of the
interface it arrived on (RFC 6762 §5.5, §11: routed here, or spoofed) is
dropped unread, and so is one the embedder's new source filter refuses
(`ServiceDaemon::new_with_source_filter`; Sukkula passes its reach policy,
private, link-local and ULA only). A response from a port other than the
daemon's is ignored (RFC 6762 §6, "MUST silently ignore"). Legacy-unicast
replies are limited to 4 a second per address, for at most 64 addresses at
once; at most 16 deferred responses wait. "Legacy" means a source port
other than the daemon's own.

Upstream's negative-answer tests send legacy queries from 127.0.0.1
faster than that; their helper now starts each query with a fresh
limiter, which local_link_tests covers on its own.

Findings: Sukkula review 2026-09-25 kept[15] (S7 on the Quick Share mDNS
responder).
