+/// Most legacy-unicast responses sent to one address per second. They go to
+/// whatever address and port the query came from, and are exempt from the
+/// once-per-second multicast limit of RFC 6762 §6, so without this a stream
+/// of small queries turns the responder into a reflector of bigger answers.
+const UNICAST_REPLIES_PER_ADDR_PER_SEC: u32 = 4;
+
+/// Most addresses [`UNICAST_REPLIES_PER_ADDR_PER_SEC`] keeps count of. When
+/// that many have been answered within the last second, nobody new is
+/// answered until one of them ages out.
+const UNICAST_REPLY_ADDRS_MAX: usize = 64;
+
+/// Most deferred PTR responses (RFC 6762 §6) waiting at once. Each is
+/// multicast, and the multicast limit lets one per record per second out
+/// anyway; beyond this many, a new one is dropped rather than queued.
+const DELAYED_RESPONSES_MAX: usize = 16;
